  "worked_solution": "The position of the particle at $t=4$ is equal to its position at $t=1$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [1,4].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(4)=x(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{4}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(4) & =12+{\\int }_{1}^{4}(4t+3{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12+[2{t}^{2}+{t}^{3}]_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =12+([2(4)^{2}+{4}^{3}]-[2(1)^{2}+{1}^{3}]) \\\\  & =12+([32+64]-[2+1]) \\\\  & =12+(96-3) \\\\  & =12+93 \\\\  & =105\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=4$ is $x=105.$",
